[[File:Rébellion d'un esclave sur un navire négrier. E. Renard, 1839. MNM La Rochelle.JPG|thumb|[[Ayotunji]], an enslaved [[W:Yoruba people|Imo]] man turned pirate, whom led a successful revolt aboard the [[W:Schooner|schooner]] [[La Almunia|''La Almunia'']]]]
{{History of Balisca}}
The '''Maroon pirates''', '''Mozarabic pirates''', or '''Maroon corsairs''' were mainly Muslim and Kaftian [[W:Piracy|pirates]] and [[W:Privateer|privateers]] who operated from the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. This area was known to [[Volisania]] as '''Satanazes''' ("Isle of Devils"), in reference to the self-liberated [[W:Maroons|Maroons]]. The main purposes of their attacks were to [[Maroon Wars|disrupt]] the [[Ethiopian slave trade|Aethiopian slave trade]], liberate the enslaved, and to seize additional ships. Their predation extended throughout the North Ethiopic, around the entirety of the east coast of [[Abyala]], and east along [[West Kaftia]]'s Ethiopic seaboard, predominately near [[Sanyan]]. In addition to seizing [[W:Merchant ship|merchant ships]], they engaged in ''[[W:Razzia (military)|razzias]]'', raids on coastal towns and villages, mainly in what is now [[Bayara]].

Maroon corsairs captured thousands of merchant ships and repeatedly raided coastal towns. As a result, residents abandoned their former villages of long stretches of coast in southern Bayara. The raids were such a problem that coastal settlements were seldom undertaken until the 19th century.

Many of these corsairs were also Illypnian outcasts and [[W:Religious conversion|converts]] (renegade), such as [[Anġlu Tirekett]] and [[Wiġi Tramblet]], [[Thermessa|Thermessan]] brothers that took control of an imperial village at the mouth of the [[Guadazequias River|Guadazequias river]] (present day [[Porciúncula]]) on behalf of the Confederacy. The Illypnian pirates brought advanced sailing and shipbuilding techniques to the Maroon Confederacy around 1640, which enabled the corsairs to extend their activities into the [[Ethiopian Ocean]]. The intensity of the Maroon piracy reached its zenith in the aftermath of the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] between [[Sanyan]] and the Maroons. Meanwhile, the effects of raids peaked in the early-to-mid-18th century.

The scope of corsair activity declined in the early 18th century, as the Volisanian Empire was split by the [[Volisanian Civil War]], and the Baliscan colonies [[Volisanya Boba|exercising their own autonomy]] for the first time. However, the ships and coasts of settler colonies without such effective protection continued to suffer until the middle of the 19th century. The conclusion of the [[Baliscan Civil War]] and the [[Northern Confederation]]'s signing of the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] resulted in the end of piracy.

